\n\nThis is worth one more command. Use grep, not python (transcript is jsonl, grep is fine; avoid cat/head per tool guidance — grep -o with head is acceptable? The guidance says avoid cat/head/tail/sed/awk/echo \"unless a dedicated tool cannot accomplish\". Grep isn't on the avoid list. `| head -50` — head is on the avoid list... The Grep tool isn't available to me directly (not in my tool list!). Wait — my tools: Agent, Bash, Edit, ListAgents, Read, ReportFindings, ScheduleWakeup, Skill, ToolSearch, Workflow, Write, web reader. No Grep/Glob. So Bash grep is the dedicated-tool-compatible route. I'll use grep with -m to limit instead of head where possible.\n\nCommand:\n`wc -l <file>; grep -no '\"command\":\"[^\"]*\"' <file> | cut -c1-200` — hmm cut is fine (not on avoid list). Actually let me do: `grep -o '\"command\":\"[^\"]*\"' /path | nl` — nl not needed; grep preserves order. Limit output: `grep -m 40 -o ...`.\n\nAlso check first line timestamp to see if history was rewritten: `grep -m1 -o '\"timestamp\":\"[^\"]*\"' file` and compare with session start 1793470003390 (≈ Oct 31 18:06). Post-deletion would be ~18:08+.\n\nLet me combine into one command:\n\n```\nf=/home/agent/.claude/projects/-workspace/86ffb1dc-33f9-4f56-be21-3fcec558218c.jsonl; wc -l \"$f\"; grep -m1 -o '\"timestamp\":\"[^\"]*\"' \"$f\"; grep -m 60 -o '\"command\":\"[^\"]*' \"$f\"\n```\n\nSemicolons fine. Output will show how many entries and all commands in order. If it shows early commands like ls of .claude dirs, great.","signature":"c37ae2e2e245427eb436097e"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"86ffb1dc-33f9-4f56-be21-3fcec558218c","uuid":"b68eca68-552f-4eb1-be75-ac9c31f69462","timestamp":"2026-10-31T18:09:45.472Z","thinking_duration_ms":13178,"thinking_display":"updates"}
